WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:05.590 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  there's no other climate quite as  inhospitable as the arctic  in the extreme north there are no trees  just sea snow and ice in this frozen  landscape the inuit people have made  their home it's a difficult life to be  sure but one they're remarkably well  adapted to to the inuit religion and  survival are intertwined failure to  follow the correct hunting rituals could  threaten the survival of your entire  tribe upset the mother of the sea sedna  and she'll withhold the ocean's seals in  her tangled hair starving you of your  primary food source dare to hunt alone  and you could risk being abducted by the  naught the monster seagull of the skies  so enter igloo and rustle up some muktuk  it's time to introduce you to inuit  mythology and the folklore of the arctic  [Music]  the inuit have historically lived  entirely above the arctic circle on the  northern seas and islands of greenland  canada alaska and parts of siberia  you're probably aware that the term  eskimo is a bit outdated it's believed  to translate to one who eats raw flesh  in algonquin understandably they prefer  to be known as inuit which simply means  people although humans have lived there  for many thousands of years the inuit  are the latest in a long line of people  groups to call the arctic their home is  believed that by the year 1100 the inuit  had replaced the previous people group  known as the dorset culture this  population shift is recorded in some  myths where the inuit speak of fierce  battles against ancestral giants known  as the tunit the first contact the inuit  had with the outside world may have been  in the viking era the icelandic  chronicle vinland saga tells the story  of eric the red's voyage to greenland it  describes how the viking explorers  encountered a strange people called the  skrelinger the skrelinger believed to be  the native inuit are depicted as being  wild and savage and were known to raid  early viking settlements in the new  world in the 1960s archaeologists found  the remains of a viking colony in  northern canada on the large island that  is coincidentally named newfoundland the  settlement however was long abandoned  the skrelinger may have chased them away  within the following centuries waves of  europeans began to settle the americas  the inuit shared a fate that many  indigenous people did to the south their  population was introduced to new  diseases that they had no natural  immunity to and survivors were forcibly  converted to christianity however  because the arctic climate is so  treacherous settlers were largely unable  to build permanent residences so far up  north the inuit already being well  adapted to these frosty conditions were  largely left alone in their ancestral  homelands as a result the beliefs  traditions and myths of the inuit have  largely survived into the modern day  before we turn onto the mythology itself  it's important to note that inuit is a  blanket term that covers a wide range of  people culture and language remember  we're talking about a massive area of  land stretching about 5000 kilometers  with such a diversity it's impossible to  talk about a unified mythology that  applies to all as we explore the  mythology of the inuit people just keep  in mind that we're drawing on a diverse  range of traditions  a key belief across inuit mythology is  that everything on earth has a life  force the word for this is inua  literally translating to person  every human has an innua but so too do  walruses whales and polar bears in this  way animals are simply different types  of people humans do not rule over the  natural world instead they exist as  equals to it when hunters catch and kill  an animal they're quite literally  killing another person therefore the  proper respect must be displayed and  there are rituals and traditions that  must be adhered to for example when a  hunter kills a polar bear it's important  to hang its skin up for a number of days  for other inuit people hunters must  place its severed head in the direction  of the body if the correct practices are  followed the inui of the polar bear will  be born into another animal or human  similar to reincarnation if any of these  rituals are not followed or done so  incorrectly it's believed that the inuit  of the animal will become angry and  reappear as a demon these demons could  cause you all kinds of trouble from the  relatively minor like bad luck to the  more life-ending like unsuccessful hunts  and blizzards it's therefore essential  to follow all the rituals and traditions  failing to do so may threaten your very  survival  the closest thing to a god or goddess in  inuit folklore are the number of nature  spirits and mythological figures perhaps  the most famous of these is the mother  of the sea known to many as sedna sedna  has a very dark origin story and there  are plenty of variations to it but they  tend to go something a bit like this  sedna was the daughter of the chief she  was the most beautiful woman in all the  tribe her hair was the longest as well  as the darkest so much so her father  attempted to marry her into the families  of powerful tribes sedna did not like  this and was not happy with any of the  men her father picked out for her in  protest she mocked her father by getting  married to a dog outraged and disgusted  sedna's father forced her into his kayak  and paddled out to sea from there he  threw her overboard into the icy water  she desperately attempted to climb back  on board but her father grabbed his  knife and sliced off her fingers her  severed digits became the first seals  unable to swim sedna sank to the bottom  of the sea and was transformed into a  vengeful sea spirit nowadays she prefers  the company of animals instead of humans  and is the guardian of marine life many  of whom enjoy nesting in her long hair  if humans fail to pay respect to the  animals that they catch sedna will  tangle them up in her hair in these  times of crisis a tribe shaman called an  angakuk will enter a trance and visit  her in their dreams apologizing  profusely to her on behalf of the tribe  and combing her hair to release the  animals  the myth of sedna underscores how  important it is to follow the many  rituals and superstitions because crops  don't grow in the arctic an inuit's diet  will consist largely of meats and animal  fats predominantly from whales and seals  if you upset sedna she'll likely drive  you and your tribe to starvation on a  less grim note in 2003 astronomers  discovered a large planet-like object  out in the cold fringes of our solar  system they decided to name it sedna in  honour of a frosty inuit spirit  [Music]  first up is the kalupalit these are half  women half scaly fish monsters that live  close by the shores they spend their  days waiting for children to stray away  from their families legend has it that  when they find a straight child they  drag it down into the icy depths and  devour them in underwater caves  you can see how this kalupulit story has  a practical purpose in daily life it's a  convenient and effective way to keep  children close by and away from the  ocean and thin ice there's also the now  jacuzzi look the so-called monster  seagull it flies about the arctic skies  looking for humans when it finds them it  swoops down catching them in its beak  and carrying them up into the air when  it reaches a high enough altitude it  will drop them down so they land on the  rocks below real seagulls do this with  crabs but this happens on a much larger  scale and finally we have the adlet the  dog people they are said to live in  tribes in northern greenland and are  known to attack humans that get too  close they'll spare the sled dogs  however and will even adopt them into  their tribe  soon it is said that the dogs will  transform into more adlets  the folklore of the arctic is immensely  practical focused on survival more than  theology or dogma there are plenty more  strange myths practices and traditions  that characterize inuit mythology so  i've included links down below if you  wish to do some further reading we'll  leave the mythology and folklore of the  arctic for now but i want to turn the  question on to you the viewer what  obscure mythology do you want to see me  cover next let me know the overlooked  and underrated mythologies and belief  systems that interest you  who knows i might cover it in a future  video  hey thanks for watching this video i  really hope you enjoyed this one if  you'd like to see more why not subscribe  a like and a comment also go a really  long way  anyway i look forward to seeing you next  time goodbye

